  "summary": "The prior rounds converge on a reasonable decomposition strategy but share a blind spot: they treat the refactor as a design problem when it's primarily a migration-safety problem. The real risk isn't choosing the wrong module boundaries—all three proposals' boundaries are defensible—it's that a mid-flight refactor of a 4,298-line file with no test coverage will silently break behavior. The file has zero unit tests for PipelineApp (test_unified_edit_modal.py tests one modal in isolation, and test_scripts.py tests scripts, but nothing exercises the action_* methods, the _check_for_changes loop, or the auto-plan/auto-implement state machines). Extracting code into new modules without a test harness means every extraction is a leap of faith that import resolution, callback wiring, and reactive attribute access still work. The pragmatic sequencing should therefore be: (0) before any extraction, write a minimal Textual app-test harness using textual's built-in pilot testing that exercises the critical paths—board switching, feature selection, action_approve flow, action_reject flow, and the auto-plan state machine—so that each subsequent extraction can be validated mechanically rather than by manual smoke-testing. This is ~2 hours of work that de-risks every subsequent step. Round 3's proposal to use Textual's message bus for the PipelineController is the right call, but underestimates a practical constraint: the _run_plan_async, _run_implement_async, and _run_ideating_async methods don't just post completion events—they mutate PipelineApp state at multiple intermediate points (setting _plan_running flags, calling _log_line dozens of times during execution, updating selected_feature mid-run when the feature file is reloaded from disk). A clean message-bus extraction would require either (a) batching these intermediate mutations into discrete state-update messages (which changes the UX timing of log output and spinner behavior) or (b) giving the controller a direct reference back to the app's reactive state (which defeats the purpose of extraction). The honest answer is that these async workers are the irreducible core that will remain in PipelineApp or a close mixin—trying to fully extract them into a separate controller is architecturally appealing but practically yields a controller that's just PipelineApp with extra indirection. A more honest decomposition accepts that PipelineApp will remain ~1,500 lines after extracting helpers (~400 lines), modals (~750 lines), panes (~300 lines), and the CLI/entry-point code (~150 lines), and that further decomposition of the async worker core requires a fundamentally different architecture (e.g., moving to Textual's Worker API properly) that should be a separate initiative. Finally, the _check_for_changes bug identified in round 3 (FeatureFile.list_all() re-statting all boards on every change) is real and worth fixing independently, but the proposed fix of caching the all-features list is fragile—a cleaner approach is to only push the active board's features to the server and let the server aggregate across boards itself, which eliminates the cross-board stat entirely and simplifies the push protocol."
